// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

// map.cla: the Map family, rewritten from a sorted parallel-array (binary
// search, O(n) 256-byte-block memmove inserts) to an open-addressed
// (linear-probe) HASHTABLE (map-hashtable phase, Task 5). The sorted
// implementation is preserved verbatim as `sortedmap of T`
// (sortedmap.cla, map-hashtable phase Tasks 1-3) for callers that need
// ascending-key iteration; plain `map of T` no longer promises any
// particular order, only that a given sequence of inserts/removes always
// replays the same (implementation-defined) order deterministically.
//
// EXACT box layout / hash / probe / growth / remove rules are normative in
// .superpowers/sdd/2026-08-10-map-hashtable/shared-design-constants.md --
// this file and runtime/host/rt_core.inc's C twin both implement that
// document verbatim, function by function, so the two lanes stay
// byte-identical. This matters beyond aesthetics: cprint.cla's
// fpUiEditStmt (the `edit F, mapVar` UI statement's map arm) and
// runtime/host/rt_ser.inc's legacy load/save fallback both call the public
// C `rt_map_*` functions directly (UNGATED by cpMapPorted/cpMapArcPorted)
// against the SAME struct instances this Clarus module's `rtMap*`
// functions create and mutate via `rtMapNew` -- a single running program
// can read/write one map through EITHER lane, so any algorithmic drift
// between them would corrupt live data, not just produce a cosmetic
// difference.
//
// New box layout (RtMap below / `struct rt_map`, rt_core.inc): 13 fields
// -- `rc`, `keys` (now an entry-META array, not raw key bytes -- see
// below), `vals` (unchanged role), `valsize`, `count`, `cap`, `valcap`,
// `keypool` (new: raw key content bytes, end-to-end), `poolused`,
// `poolcap`, `index` (new: the open-addressing slot table), `indexcap`,
// `tombs`. rt_core.inc's own rt_map_layout_check typedef guards this
// overlay and the real C struct can never drift, same placement/reasoning
// as before.
//
// Entries are DENSE: `keys` holds `count` fixed 12-byte meta records
// {hash:int32, keyOff:int32, keyLen:int32} (MAP_ENTRY_STRIDE), index-
// aligned with `vals`'s `count` fixed-`valsize` value slots -- same
// index-alignment relationship the old sorted-array design had between
// `keys` and `vals`, just with `keys` holding meta instead of full Str255
// blocks now. A key's actual bytes live in `keypool`, referenced by each
// entry's own (keyOff, keyLen) -- appended once (mapPoolAppend below) and
// never moved or reclaimed by a later remove (an abandoned key's pool
// bytes are simply dead weight until the whole map is released; the
// shared design constants document this "abandon on remove" rule
// explicitly, favoring an append-only pool over compaction complexity).
//
// `index` is the open-addressed lookup structure: `indexcap` (a power of
// two, 0 until the very first insert) int32 slots, each holding an entry
// index (>= 0), EMPTY (-1), or TOMB (-2, a removed entry's former slot,
// kept so a later probe chain doesn't terminate early). mapHash (below)
// computes a key's hash (djb2, masked to 31 bits after EVERY step -- see
// its own doc comment); mapIndexFindSlot/mapIndexLookup/mapIndexSlotFor/
// mapIndexInsert/mapIndexRepoint (below) implement the probe/match/insert/
// repoint rules exactly as the shared design constants document. Growth:
// mapEnsureIndexCapacity materializes indexcap=8 on the very first insert,
// then mapRehash doubles + rebuilds whenever `(count + tombs) * 4 >=
// indexcap * 3` (checked, per the shared design constants, using the
// state BEFORE the pending insert) -- entries/vals/keypool grow via the
// same amortized-doubling SetHandleSize pattern the old rtMapGrowKeys/
// rtMapGrowVals already used (mapGrowEntries/mapGrowVals/mapGrowPool
// below), unrelated to the index's own doubling.
//
// Remove is swap-with-last: TOMB the removed entry's index slot, then (if
// it wasn't already the last dense entry) copy the last entry's meta+value
// into the removed slot and re-point the MOVED entry's own index slot from
// its old dense position to its new one (mapIndexRepoint below finds that
// slot by matching the moved entry's OWN stored index value along its
// hash's probe chain -- entry indices are unique among live entries, so
// this is unambiguous and equivalent to, but simpler than, re-deriving and
// comparing the moved entry's key bytes; see mapIndexRepoint's own doc
// comment for why this is a deliberate simplification over the shared
// design constants' literal "find it by ... key compare" phrasing, and why
// it's still exactly reproducible between the two lanes). Iteration
// (rtMapKeyAt/rtMapValAt) walks the dense arrays 0..count-1 directly, so
// it visits entries in insertion order, perturbed by any removes --
// unspecified but deterministic, never sorted.
//
// RC boundary (unchanged from the sorted implementation, Plan 5c wave2a
// Task 4): birth (rtMapNew) constructs the WHOLE box itself (MapNewPtr +
// overlay pokes + FOUR MapNewHandle(0) calls: keys, vals, keypool, index);
// rtMapRetain/rtMapRelease do the rc arithmetic and the rc==0 dispose
// sequence here too, calling MapRcCheck only on the cold over-release path
// (`rc <= 0`); rtMapLastref (`m != NULL and m.rc == 1`) is the null-safe
// "am I the only owner" predicate cpEmitRelease's element-release walk
// gates on. rtMapRelease's rc==0 path now disposes FOUR handles before the
// box (keys, vals, keypool, index, in that order) -- the same "extend the
// existing dispose order" rule Task 5's brief calls for; every other ARC
// wiring detail (cpMapArcPorted's redirect scope, fpUiEditStmt's STAYS-C
// exclusion, cpMapPorted's own separate accessor gate) is UNCHANGED by
// this rewrite -- see git history for the fuller account this header
// comment used to carry about that wiring, still accurate today.
//
// Relocation discipline (UNCHANGED, still load-bearing): `keys`, `vals`,
// `keypool`, and `index` are FOUR separate Handles, each grown and
// potentially moved independently. Every access to any of them re-derives
// that Handle's master pointer via MapHandleDeref on EVERY call (no
// caching of a dereferenced pointer across any call that can allocate/move
// memory) -- mapEntrySlot/mapValSlot/mapIndexSlot (below) are the fresh-
// derivation points, mirroring the old mapKeySlot/mapValSlot exactly, now
// joined by a third for the index and a fourth (inline in mapPoolAppend/
// mapPoolKeyEq/rtMapKeyAt) for the keypool. The paranoid allocator
// (CLARUS_MEM_PARANOID) catches violations of this rule in the leak-gated
// test runs. Key/value/meta block moves go through MapBlockMoveData (a
// BlockMoveData/memmove, not memcpy) with source/destination ranges
// computed from freshly-derived slot pointers.
//
// Behavior contract (UNCHANGED from the sorted implementation): `get` on
// an absent key panics ("map key not found"); `get_dv` returns false (out
// untouched) on absent, true (out filled) on present; `remove` is silent
// on an absent key; `key_at`/`val_at` panic ("map key not found") on an
// out-of-range index; `clear` resets count to 0 (also tombs to 0, poolused
// to 0, and every index slot to EMPTY), keeping every Handle's capacity --
// same interface-parity reasoning as before (still unreachable from any
// emitted program today, no `IMapClear` intrinsic exists).
//
// MapHandleDeref/MapSetHandleSize/MapBlockMoveData/MapNewPtr/
// MapDisposePtr/MapNewHandle/MapDisposeHandle/MapRcCheck are unchanged
// from the sorted implementation -- same externs, same name-collision
// reasoning (distinct Map-prefixed names sidestep checkFuncSig's
// name-only collision hazard), same rt_ext_Map* wrapper backing in both
// runtime/host/rt_ext_host.inc and runtime/mac/rt_ext_mac.inc.
external func MapHandleDeref(h: ptr): ptr = inline deref
// `reg memerr`, not plain `reg` -- see text.cla's own TextSetHandleSize
// doc comment (Task 14, native-5e) for why: SetHandleSize is a void
// Pascal procedure, no result in D0.
external func MapSetHandleSize(h: ptr, n: int): int = trap 0xA024 reg memerr
external func MapBlockMoveData(src: ptr, dst: ptr, count: int) = trap 0xA22E reg
external func MapNewPtr(size: int): ptr = trap 0xA11E reg
external func MapDisposePtr(p: ptr) = trap 0xA01F reg
external func MapNewHandle(n: int): ptr = trap 0xA122 reg
external func MapDisposeHandle(h: ptr) = trap 0xA023 reg
external func MapRcCheck(box: ptr, rc: int) = inline nop

// MAP_ENTRY_STRIDE: byte width of one entry's meta record in `keys` --
// {hash:int32, keyOff:int32, keyLen:int32} (shared design constants' own
// "Entry meta stride" rule). Offsets within a record: hash at +0, keyOff
// at +4, keyLen at +8.
const MAP_ENTRY_STRIDE: int = 12

// INDEX_EMPTY/INDEX_TOMB: the two negative sentinel values an `index` slot
// can hold besides a non-negative entry index (shared design constants'
// "Index slot values" rule). A fresh or just-grown index's slots are all
// INDEX_EMPTY.
const INDEX_EMPTY: int = -1
const INDEX_TOMB: int = -2

// RtMap (Ch13: Overlay Records) mirrors rt_core.inc's `struct rt_map`
// field-for-field (13 fields) -- rt_core.inc's rt_map_layout_check
// typedef guards this layout stays exact.
overlay record RtMap {
    rc: int
    keys: ptr
    vals: ptr
    valsize: int
    count: int
    cap: int
    valcap: int
    keypool: ptr
    poolused: int
    poolcap: int
    index: ptr
    indexcap: int
    tombs: int
}

// mapEntrySlot: the address of entry i's 12-byte meta record -- derives
// the keys Handle's master pointer fresh on every call (relocation
// discipline, module header comment above).
func mapEntrySlot(m: ptr, i: int): ptr {
    return MapHandleDeref(RtMap(m).keys) + i * MAP_ENTRY_STRIDE
}

// mapValSlot: the address of value entry i -- same role/discipline as the
// old sorted implementation's own mapValSlot.
func mapValSlot(m: ptr, i: int): ptr {
    return MapHandleDeref(RtMap(m).vals) + i * RtMap(m).valsize
}

// mapIndexSlot: the address of index slot i (one int32 each) -- derives
// the index Handle's master pointer fresh on every call.
func mapIndexSlot(m: ptr, i: int): ptr {
    return MapHandleDeref(RtMap(m).index) + i * 4
}

// mapEntryHash/mapEntryKeyOff/mapEntryKeyLen: read the three fields of
// entry i's meta record.
func mapEntryHash(m: ptr, i: int): int {
    return peekl(mapEntrySlot(m, i))
}
func mapEntryKeyOff(m: ptr, i: int): int {
    return peekl(mapEntrySlot(m, i) + 4)
}
func mapEntryKeyLen(m: ptr, i: int): int {
    return peekl(mapEntrySlot(m, i) + 8)
}

// mapSetEntryMeta writes all three fields of entry i's meta record in one
// call (a single freshly-derived slot address, then three pokes).
func mapSetEntryMeta(m: ptr, i: int, hash: int, keyOff: int, keyLen: int) {
    var slot: ptr

    slot = mapEntrySlot(m, i)
    pokel(slot, hash)
    pokel(slot + 4, keyOff)
    pokel(slot + 8, keyLen)
}

// mapHash (shared design constants' "Hash (map)" rule): djb2, masked to
// 31 bits after EVERY step, not just at the end -- `h = 5381; for each
// byte b: h = (((h << 5) + h) + b) and 0x7FFFFFFF`. Clarus's bitwise-AND
// operator is `&` (`and`/`or` are the short-circuit BOOLEAN operators,
// Ch4) -- the shared design constants' "and 0x7FFFFFFF" pseudocode is
// this same mask, spelled with `&` here. `key` is a Pascal-string pointer
// (peekb(key) = length, key+1.. = content bytes), the same shape every
// other map key parameter in this file uses.
func mapHash(key: ptr): int {
    var klen: int
    var i: int
    var h: int
    var b: int

    klen = peekb(key)
    h = 5381
    i = 0
    while i < klen {
        b = peekb(key + 1 + i)
        h = (h << 5) + h + b
        h = h & 0x7FFFFFFF
        i = i + 1
    }
    return h
}

// mapPoolKeyEq (shared design constants' "Match test (map)" rule, the
// keyLen/byte-compare half -- the hash compare happens at each call site,
// against the entry's own stored hash, before this is even called):
// compares a candidate entry's own (keyOff, keyLen) pool range against
// `key`/`klen` (a Pascal-string pointer + its length). keyLen != klen
// short-circuits before any byte is touched.
func mapPoolKeyEq(m: ptr, keyOff: int, keyLen: int, key: ptr, klen: int): bool {
    var base: ptr
    var i: int

    if keyLen != klen {
        return false
    }
    base = MapHandleDeref(RtMap(m).keypool) + keyOff
    i = 0
    while i < klen {
        if peekb(base + i) != peekb(key + 1 + i) {
            return false
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return true
}

// mapIndexFindSlot is the one shared probe loop mapIndexLookup (entry
// index) and rtMapRemove (needs the INDEX SLOT itself, to TOMB it) both
// build on -- shared design constants' "Probe" + "Match test" rules in
// one place: starting at `hash & (indexcap - 1)`, step `+1 & (indexcap -
// 1)` (linear); an EMPTY slot ends the search (return -1, key absent); a
// TOMB slot is skipped; an occupied slot is a match iff its stored hash
// equals `hash` AND its stored key equals `key` (mapPoolKeyEq). Returns
// -1 immediately, without probing, when indexcap == 0 (nothing has ever
// been inserted).
func mapIndexFindSlot(m: ptr, hash: int, key: ptr): int {
    var indexcap: int
    var klen: int
    var slot: int
    var v: int

    indexcap = RtMap(m).indexcap
    if indexcap == 0 {
        return -1
    }
    klen = peekb(key)
    slot = hash & (indexcap - 1)
    while true {
        v = peekl(mapIndexSlot(m, slot))
        if v == INDEX_EMPTY {
            return -1
        }
        if v != INDEX_TOMB {
            if mapEntryHash(m, v) == hash and mapPoolKeyEq(m, mapEntryKeyOff(m, v), mapEntryKeyLen(m, v), key, klen) {
                return slot
            }
        }
        slot = (slot + 1) & (indexcap - 1)
    }
}

// mapIndexLookup (Interfaces: mapIndexLookup(m, hash, key) -> entryIdx or
// -1): a thin wrapper over mapIndexFindSlot that reads the matched slot's
// stored entry index. Used by Get/GetDv/Has/Set's upsert check.
func mapIndexLookup(m: ptr, hash: int, key: ptr): int {
    var slot: int

    slot = mapIndexFindSlot(m, hash, key)
    if slot < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    return peekl(mapIndexSlot(m, slot))
}

// mapIndexSlotFor (Interfaces: mapIndexSlotFor(m, hash, key) -> slot, "the
// insert position, reusing first tombstone"): probes the same sequence
// mapIndexFindSlot would, remembering the first TOMB slot seen; returns
// that reused TOMB slot if one was seen, else the terminating EMPTY slot.
// Callers only ever call this once the key is already known absent
// (rtMapSet, after its own mapIndexLookup missed) or where reinsertion is
// by a live entry's own already-unique stored hash (mapRehash, where no
// two live entries can collide on both hash AND key) -- so, unlike
// mapIndexFindSlot, this never needs to compare `key` against an occupied
// slot's stored key; `key` is accepted (not dropped) only to keep the same
// call shape mapIndexLookup uses, matching the shared design constants'
// Interfaces list exactly (rt_core.inc's C twin, map_index_slot_for, does
// the same). mapRehash passes ptr(0) for `key` at its own call site.
func mapIndexSlotFor(m: ptr, hash: int, key: ptr): int {
    var indexcap: int
    var slot: int
    var firstTomb: int
    var v: int

    indexcap = RtMap(m).indexcap
    slot = hash & (indexcap - 1)
    firstTomb = -1
    while true {
        v = peekl(mapIndexSlot(m, slot))
        if v == INDEX_EMPTY {
            if firstTomb >= 0 {
                return firstTomb
            }
            return slot
        }
        if v == INDEX_TOMB and firstTomb < 0 {
            firstTomb = slot
        }
        slot = (slot + 1) & (indexcap - 1)
    }
}

// mapIndexInsert (Interfaces: mapIndexInsert(m, hash, entryIdx)): writes
// entryIdx into the slot mapIndexSlotFor finds for `hash`, decrementing
// `tombs` if that slot was a reused TOMB (the one place tombs bookkeeping
// happens on the insert side -- mapRehash's own tombs=0 reset means this
// branch never actually fires during a rehash, since every slot it writes
// into starts fresh EMPTY).
func mapIndexInsert(m: ptr, hash: int, entryIdx: int) {
    var rm: RtMap
    var slot: int
    var slotPtr: ptr
    var old: int

    rm = RtMap(m)
    slot = mapIndexSlotFor(m, hash, ptr(0))
    slotPtr = mapIndexSlot(m, slot)
    old = peekl(slotPtr)
    pokel(slotPtr, entryIdx)
    if old == INDEX_TOMB {
        rm.tombs = rm.tombs - 1
    }
}

// mapIndexRepoint: after rtMapRemove copies the last dense entry down into
// the removed entry's old slot, the MOVED entry's own index slot (which
// still holds its OLD dense position, `oldIdx`) must be updated to point
// at its NEW position, `newIdx`. This probes `hash`'s sequence (the moved
// entry's own stored hash) for the slot whose CURRENT value is exactly
// `oldIdx` -- entry indices are unique among live entries, so this is an
// unambiguous match, simpler than (but equivalent to) re-deriving the
// moved entry's key bytes and comparing them the way mapIndexFindSlot
// does: whichever slot mapIndexInsert originally wrote `oldIdx` into for
// this same hash is the one and only slot this probe can find holding
// that value, by construction. A deliberate simplification over the
// shared design constants' literal "find it by ... key compare" phrasing,
// applied identically in rt_core.inc's C twin (map_index_repoint) so both
// lanes stay in lockstep regardless.
func mapIndexRepoint(m: ptr, hash: int, oldIdx: int, newIdx: int) {
    var indexcap: int
    var slot: int
    var v: int

    indexcap = RtMap(m).indexcap
    slot = hash & (indexcap - 1)
    while true {
        v = peekl(mapIndexSlot(m, slot))
        if v == oldIdx {
            pokel(mapIndexSlot(m, slot), newIdx)
            return
        }
        slot = (slot + 1) & (indexcap - 1)
    }
}

// mapAllocIndex (re)sizes the index Handle to `newcap` int32 slots and
// resets every slot to EMPTY -- used both to materialize the index on the
// very first insert (indexcap 0 -> 8, mapEnsureIndexCapacity below) and,
// from mapRehash, to grow it to double the old capacity before rebuilding.
func mapAllocIndex(m: ptr, newcap: int) {
    var rm: RtMap
    var err: int
    var mp: ptr
    var i: int

    rm = RtMap(m)
    err = MapSetHandleSize(rm.index, newcap * 4)
    if err != 0 {
        rtPanic("out of memory")
    }
    rm.indexcap = newcap
    mp = MapHandleDeref(rm.index)
    i = 0
    while i < newcap {
        pokel(mp + i * 4, INDEX_EMPTY)
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// mapRehash (shared design constants' "Growth" rule, the doubling half):
// doubles indexcap, resets every slot to EMPTY (via mapAllocIndex), resets
// tombs to 0, then re-inserts every live dense entry (0..count-1) by its
// OWN stored hash -- no key comparison needed (mapIndexSlotFor's own doc
// comment above), so `key` is ptr(0) at every mapIndexInsert call this
// makes.
func mapRehash(m: ptr) {
    var rm: RtMap
    var newcap: int
    var i: int
    var h: int

    rm = RtMap(m)
    newcap = rm.indexcap * 2
    mapAllocIndex(m, newcap)
    rm = RtMap(m)
    rm.tombs = 0
    i = 0
    while i < rm.count {
        h = mapEntryHash(m, i)
        mapIndexInsert(m, h, i)
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// mapEnsureIndexCapacity: materializes the index (indexcap 8, all EMPTY)
// on the very first insert (indexcap == 0), or rehash-doubles it once the
// shared design constants' load-factor threshold is crossed: `(count +
// tombs) * 4 >= indexcap * 3`, checked (per that same document) using the
// state BEFORE the pending insert. Called by rtMapSet before every INSERT
// (never before an overwrite -- an overwrite touches no index slot at
// all).
func mapEnsureIndexCapacity(m: ptr) {
    var rm: RtMap

    rm = RtMap(m)
    if rm.indexcap == 0 {
        mapAllocIndex(m, 8)
        return
    }
    if (rm.count + rm.tombs) * 4 >= rm.indexcap * 3 {
        mapRehash(m)
    }
}

// mapGrowEntries (Interfaces: mapGrowEntries(m, need)) -- the entry-meta
// array's own amortized-doubling grower, same shape as the old sorted
// implementation's rtMapGrowKeys (doubles cap from 4 if currently empty,
// until it covers `need`, then MapSetHandleSize once, in bytes,
// newcap*MAP_ENTRY_STRIDE). Callers that hold a master pointer derived
// before calling this MUST re-derive it afterward (relocation discipline,
// module header comment above) -- growth can move the block.
func mapGrowEntries(m: ptr, need: int) {
    var rm: RtMap
    var cap: int
    var newcap: int
    var err: int

    rm = RtMap(m)
    cap = rm.cap
    if need <= cap {
        return
    }
    if cap > 0 {
        newcap = cap
    } else {
        newcap = 4
    }
    while newcap < need {
        newcap = newcap * 2
    }
    err = MapSetHandleSize(rm.keys, newcap * MAP_ENTRY_STRIDE)
    if err != 0 {
        rtPanic("out of memory")
    }
    rm.cap = newcap
}

// mapGrowVals: the vals Handle's own amortized-doubling grower --
// otherwise identical in structure to mapGrowEntries above (unchanged role
// and shape from the old sorted implementation's rtMapGrowVals).
func mapGrowVals(m: ptr, need: int) {
    var rm: RtMap
    var cap: int
    var newcap: int
    var valsize: int
    var err: int

    rm = RtMap(m)
    cap = rm.valcap
    if need <= cap {
        return
    }
    valsize = rm.valsize
    if cap > 0 {
        newcap = cap
    } else {
        newcap = 4
    }
    while newcap < need {
        newcap = newcap * 2
    }
    err = MapSetHandleSize(rm.vals, newcap * valsize)
    if err != 0 {
        rtPanic("out of memory")
    }
    rm.valcap = newcap
}

// mapGrowPool: the keypool Handle's own amortized-doubling grower, in
// BYTES rather than entries -- same doubling-from-4 shape as
// mapGrowEntries/mapGrowVals (shared design constants: "Entry/vals/
// keypool arrays grow by the existing doubling-SetHandleSize pattern").
func mapGrowPool(m: ptr, need: int) {
    var rm: RtMap
    var cap: int
    var newcap: int
    var err: int

    rm = RtMap(m)
    cap = rm.poolcap
    if need <= cap {
        return
    }
    if cap > 0 {
        newcap = cap
    } else {
        newcap = 4
    }
    while newcap < need {
        newcap = newcap * 2
    }
    err = MapSetHandleSize(rm.keypool, newcap)
    if err != 0 {
        rtPanic("out of memory")
    }
    rm.poolcap = newcap
}

// mapPoolAppend (Interfaces: mapPoolAppend(m, key) -> keyOff): grows the
// keypool (mapGrowPool) to fit one more key's CONTENT bytes if needed,
// then appends them (NOT the Pascal length byte -- keyLen already lives in
// the entry's own meta record; shared design constants: "raw key bytes
// end-to-end") at the current poolused offset, advances poolused by klen,
// and returns the offset the bytes were written at.
func mapPoolAppend(m: ptr, key: ptr): int {
    var rm: RtMap
    var klen: int
    var off: int
    var mp: ptr

    klen = peekb(key)
    rm = RtMap(m)
    mapGrowPool(m, rm.poolused + klen)
    rm = RtMap(m)
    off = rm.poolused
    mp = MapHandleDeref(rm.keypool)
    MapBlockMoveData(key + 1, mp + off, klen)
    rm.poolused = off + klen
    return off
}

// rtMapBoxSize is MapNewPtr's argument for rtMapNew's box below. The box
// carries 13 real fields (RtMap above); this constant conservatively
// budgets 14 x 8-byte slots (112 bytes) -- one slot more than the exact
// field count -- for the same "a little slack here is harmless" reason
// the old 56-byte constant (7 fields x 8) already documented: MapNewPtr
// only guarantees "at least this many bytes," so slightly over-budgeting
// is harmless, unlike an UNDER-sized box, which would corrupt whatever
// heap memory follows it. (The shared design constants document states
// this same 112-byte figure as "14 fields x 8-byte slots" -- the extra
// slot beyond the 13 real fields is exactly this deliberate slack, not a
// miscount.)
const rtMapBoxSize: int = 112

// rtMapNew: birth allocation is entirely Clarus-owned (rc=1, valsize/
// count/cap/valcap/poolused/poolcap/indexcap/tombs poked, FOUR fresh empty
// Handles: keys, vals, keypool, index). indexcap starts at 0 -- the index
// itself is materialized on the very first insert (mapEnsureIndexCapacity
// above), not here. MapNewPtr/MapNewHandle's own C shim panics on NULL
// (host only), so these guards are dead on host, live on native -- same
// "out of memory" message the C shim uses.
func rtMapNew(valsize: int): ptr {
    var m: ptr
    var rm: RtMap

    m = MapNewPtr(rtMapBoxSize)
    if m == ptr(0) {
        rtPanic("out of memory")
    }
    rm = RtMap(m)
    rm.rc = 1
    rm.keys = MapNewHandle(0)
    if rm.keys == ptr(0) {
        rtPanic("out of memory")
    }
    rm.vals = MapNewHandle(0)
    if rm.vals == ptr(0) {
        rtPanic("out of memory")
    }
    rm.valsize = valsize
    rm.count = 0
    rm.cap = 0
    rm.valcap = 0
    rm.keypool = MapNewHandle(0)
    if rm.keypool == ptr(0) {
        rtPanic("out of memory")
    }
    rm.poolused = 0
    rm.poolcap = 0
    rm.index = MapNewHandle(0)
    if rm.index == ptr(0) {
        rtPanic("out of memory")
    }
    rm.indexcap = 0
    rm.tombs = 0
    return m
}

// rtMapRetain: null-safe increment, no over-release guard -- unchanged
// from the sorted implementation.
func rtMapRetain(m: ptr) {
    var rm: RtMap

    if m == ptr(0) {
        return
    }
    rm = RtMap(m)
    rm.rc = rm.rc + 1
}

// rtMapRelease: null-safe; the over-release guard runs ONLY on the cold
// `rc <= 0` path; decrements regardless; disposes FOUR handles then the
// box itself once rc reaches 0 -- keys, vals, keypool, index, box, in that
// order (extends the old three-handle dispose order with the two new
// ones, appended at the end).
func rtMapRelease(m: ptr) {
    var rm: RtMap

    if m == ptr(0) {
        return
    }
    rm = RtMap(m)
    if rm.rc <= 0 {
        MapRcCheck(m, rm.rc)
    }
    rm.rc = rm.rc - 1
    if rm.rc == 0 {
        MapDisposeHandle(rm.keys)
        MapDisposeHandle(rm.vals)
        MapDisposeHandle(rm.keypool)
        MapDisposeHandle(rm.index)
        MapDisposePtr(m)
    }
}

// rtMapLastref: null-safe "is this the box's only live reference"
// predicate -- unchanged from the sorted implementation.
func rtMapLastref(m: ptr): bool {
    return m != ptr(0) and RtMap(m).rc == 1
}

// rtMapSet: upsert. mapIndexLookup finds an existing match first (an
// overwrite touches only the value slot, no index/entry/pool mutation at
// all). On a miss: mapEnsureIndexCapacity (materialize or rehash-grow the
// index BEFORE this insert), grow the entries/vals arrays for one more
// entry, append the key's content bytes to the pool, write the new dense
// entry's meta + value at slot `count`, insert the new entry into the
// index, then increment count.
func rtMapSet(m: ptr, key: ptr, val: ptr) {
    var rm: RtMap
    var h: int
    var idx: int
    var klen: int
    var keyOff: int

    rm = RtMap(m)
    h = mapHash(key)
    idx = mapIndexLookup(m, h, key)
    if idx >= 0 {
        MapBlockMoveData(val, mapValSlot(m, idx), rm.valsize)
        return
    }

    mapEnsureIndexCapacity(m)
    mapGrowEntries(m, rm.count + 1)
    mapGrowVals(m, rm.count + 1)

    klen = peekb(key)
    keyOff = mapPoolAppend(m, key)

    idx = rm.count
    mapSetEntryMeta(m, idx, h, keyOff, klen)
    MapBlockMoveData(val, mapValSlot(m, idx), rm.valsize)
    mapIndexInsert(m, h, idx)
    rm.count = rm.count + 1
}

// rtMapGet: panics ("map key not found") on an absent key -- never a
// sentinel return, matching the behavior contract exactly.
func rtMapGet(m: ptr, key: ptr, out: ptr) {
    var idx: int

    idx = mapIndexLookup(m, mapHash(key), key)
    if idx < 0 {
        rtPanic("map key not found")
    }
    MapBlockMoveData(mapValSlot(m, idx), out, RtMap(m).valsize)
}

// rtMapGetDv: returns false and leaves `out` untouched on an absent key;
// returns true and fills `out` on present.
func rtMapGetDv(m: ptr, key: ptr, out: ptr): bool {
    var idx: int

    idx = mapIndexLookup(m, mapHash(key), key)
    if idx < 0 {
        return false
    }
    MapBlockMoveData(mapValSlot(m, idx), out, RtMap(m).valsize)
    return true
}

// rtMapHas.
func rtMapHas(m: ptr, key: ptr): bool {
    return mapIndexLookup(m, mapHash(key), key) >= 0
}

// rtMapRemove: silent on an absent key. On a match: TOMB the found index
// slot (mapIndexFindSlot gives the SLOT, not just the entry index, so it
// can be overwritten directly), increment tombs, then (unless the removed
// entry was already the last dense entry) copy the last entry's meta+
// value down into the removed slot and re-point the moved entry's own
// index slot (mapIndexRepoint) from its old dense position to its new
// one, before decrementing count.
func rtMapRemove(m: ptr, key: ptr) {
    var rm: RtMap
    var h: int
    var slot: int
    var idx: int
    var lastIdx: int
    var lastHash: int

    rm = RtMap(m)
    h = mapHash(key)
    slot = mapIndexFindSlot(m, h, key)
    if slot < 0 {
        return
    }
    idx = peekl(mapIndexSlot(m, slot))
    pokel(mapIndexSlot(m, slot), INDEX_TOMB)
    rm.tombs = rm.tombs + 1

    lastIdx = rm.count - 1
    if idx != lastIdx {
        MapBlockMoveData(mapEntrySlot(m, lastIdx), mapEntrySlot(m, idx), MAP_ENTRY_STRIDE)
        MapBlockMoveData(mapValSlot(m, lastIdx), mapValSlot(m, idx), rm.valsize)
        lastHash = mapEntryHash(m, idx)
        mapIndexRepoint(m, lastHash, lastIdx, idx)
    }
    rm.count = rm.count - 1
}

// rtMapCount.
func rtMapCount(m: ptr): int {
    return RtMap(m).count
}

// rtMapClear (shared design constants' "clear" rule): count = 0, tombs =
// 0, poolused = 0, every index slot reset to EMPTY -- every Handle's own
// capacity kept. Still unreachable from any emitted program today (no
// `IMapClear` intrinsic exists), kept for interface parity same as
// before.
func rtMapClear(m: ptr) {
    var rm: RtMap
    var mp: ptr
    var i: int

    rm = RtMap(m)
    rm.count = 0
    rm.tombs = 0
    rm.poolused = 0
    if rm.indexcap > 0 {
        mp = MapHandleDeref(rm.index)
        i = 0
        while i < rm.indexcap {
            pokel(mp + i * 4, INDEX_EMPTY)
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
}

// rtMapKeyAt (shared design constants' "Iteration contract" rule): panics
// ("map key not found") on an out-of-range index. Copies the length byte
// + that many content bytes from the keypool into the caller's Str255
// (key255) -- a variable-length copy now, unlike the old sorted
// implementation's fixed-256-byte block copy; fpForMapStmt's own call
// site passes the address of a `cv_<name>` Str255-typed loop variable,
// whose bytes past `1 + keyLen` are simply left whatever they already
// were (never read by any correct caller, since keyLen is always read
// alongside them).
func rtMapKeyAt(m: ptr, i: int, key255: ptr) {
    var rm: RtMap
    var keyLen: int
    var keyOff: int
    var poolBase: ptr

    rm = RtMap(m)
    if i < 0 or i >= rm.count {
        rtPanic("map key not found")
    }
    keyLen = mapEntryKeyLen(m, i)
    keyOff = mapEntryKeyOff(m, i)
    pokeb(key255, keyLen)
    poolBase = MapHandleDeref(rm.keypool)
    MapBlockMoveData(poolBase + keyOff, key255 + 1, keyLen)
}

// rtMapValAt: same out-of-range contract as rtMapKeyAt above.
func rtMapValAt(m: ptr, i: int, out: ptr) {
    var rm: RtMap

    rm = RtMap(m)
    if i < 0 or i >= rm.count {
        rtPanic("map key not found")
    }
    MapBlockMoveData(mapValSlot(m, i), out, rm.valsize)
}
